5.2 Source attribution
Every audience CTA passes a source parameter to its destination page, e.g., /provider?source=connect-provider. Downstream Remix signup flows preserve this attribution, so a provider who scans a card, taps Provider on /connect, lands on /provider, and eventually signs up will carry the connect-provider attribution all the way through to the database.
5.3 Key metrics to watch
    • Picker conversion rate: audience_selected events ÷ /connect pageviews. Healthy target ≥80%. Lower means the picker is unclear or the page is mis-loading.
    • Audience mix: the distribution of selections across the six tracks. Confirms whether the audience hypothesis is right. Surprises here are interesting — if Investor outpaces Provider in raw selections, that's signal.
    • Bridge-to-destination CTR: cta_clicked events ÷ /connect/[audience] pageviews, per audience. Healthy target ≥70%. Lower for any specific audience suggests their bridge page copy needs work.
    • Bounce rate: page_bounce events ÷ /connect pageviews. Should be low. Spikes correlate with low-quality scan sources or QR codes that ended up somewhere unexpected.
6. Brand and design rules
The /connect system follows the existing USRad dual-color brand strategy. The full document lives in the project knowledge folder. The key constraints that apply to /connect specifically:
    • Navy dominant. Hero band, primary CTAs, link colors, focus rings — all navy (#003087).
    • Gold as accent only. Used for thin rules under brand statements, accent borders on proof callouts, and small visual punctuation. Never used for CTAs (per design contract §8.1) and never used on payment surfaces.
    • Inter throughout. Site continuity wins over card continuity. The wordmark on the card uses a serif accent; the wordmark on /connect uses Inter Bold.
    • No imagery, no Lottie, no stock photos. The page must feel like signal, not decoration. Whitespace is part of the design.
    • Tap targets ≥56px. Audience cards on mobile are approximately 88px tall, well above the floor.
    • No animations beyond color transitions. Per design contract §15.3: no animate-pulse, bounce, scale-on-hover, parallax, or infinite loops. Hover and focus states use ≤300ms color transitions only.
7. Maintenance
7.1 Common tasks
Updating audience copy
Edit src/data/connectAudiences.ts. The file is structured as one object per audience with named fields (headline, body, proofLine, cta, etc.). Save and commit. Both the /connect picker and the relevant bridge page pick up the change automatically.
Adding a new audience track
    • Step 1: Add a new entry to CONNECT_AUDIENCES in src/data/connectAudiences.ts.
    • Step 2: Add the new audience ID to the AUDIENCE_ORDER array (this controls the picker's display order).
    • Step 3: Add the new ID to the AudienceId union type at the top of the file.
    • Step 4: Create src/pages/connect/[newaudience].astro by copying any existing bridge page and changing the audience reference.
TypeScript will catch errors if the union type and the data object disagree, so the addition is safe at build time.
Changing a CTA destination
Edit the cta.href field for the relevant audience in connectAudiences.ts. Maintain the ?source=connect-{audience} query parameter convention so attribution stays intact.
Retiring the /connect system
If /connect is ever retired, the rollback is clean: delete src/pages/connect.astro, the src/pages/connect/ directory, the src/components/connect/ directory, and src/data/connectAudiences.ts. The CarbonLayout slot enhancement and the /partner Health Plans & Payors addition both have independent value and should be left in place.
7.2 Things not to break
    • The brand statement on /connect must remain verbatim from the business card. "Building the nationwide marketplace for diagnostic imaging." Do not paraphrase, shorten, or split. This is the explicit promise the card made and the page is here to confirm.
    • All seven pages must remain noindex. These pages exist for QR scanners, not search traffic. Search-indexing /connect would dilute the brand promise.
    • The QR codes on printed cards must continue to resolve to usrad.com/connect. If /connect ever moves, redirect from /connect to the new location at the web server level so already-printed cards keep working.
    • GA4 event names are part of the contract. Renaming audience_selected, cta_clicked, or page_bounce breaks any saved reports or dashboards built on top of them. If a rename is needed, dual-fire the old and new event for a month first.
8. Open items and known limitations
8.1 Acknowledged limitations
    • Broker routes to /employer as a temporary stopgap. There is no dedicated /broker page yet. Brokers and employers share enough vocabulary that the employer page won't feel wrong to a broker, but a dedicated broker landing page is the better long-term answer.
    • Payor routes to /partner, but /partner uses the older site design language. When a payor scanning a premium navy-and-gold card lands on /partner, the visual continuity breaks slightly. /partner should be brought into design-contract conformance in a separate pass.
    • The global Lottie loader still loads on /connect (~250KB). This is a pre-existing performance issue across all CarbonLayout pages (engineering report H3) and is not specific to /connect. Out of scope for this work but worth fixing globally.
8.2 Future enhancements worth considering
    • Dedicated broker page. Once volume warrants it. Update src/data/connectAudiences.ts to route there instead of /employer.
    • Per-card QR codes. Currently every card routes to /connect. A future iteration could give each card a unique QR (e.g., /connect?ref=michael) for per-card attribution — "how many leads came from Michael's cards vs Donna's?"
    • Geographic detection on /connect. If we know the scan happened at a specific conference, the picker could lead with the audience most likely to attend that conference.
    • OG tag specificity per /connect page. The new CarbonLayout head slot makes per-page OG possible. Useful if /connect URLs ever get shared on LinkedIn or similar.
9. Strategic context
The premise behind this system is that the business card is the only physical artifact USRad will hand to a sophisticated counterparty before they form an opinion of the company. A premium card costs roughly two dollars to produce. The first impression it creates is worth orders of magnitude more than that — but only if the digital experience on the other side of the QR code matches the physical quality of the card.
A cheap card with a generic homepage on the other side reads as inconsistent. A premium card with a generic homepage on the other side is worse — the contrast itself becomes the message: "we cared about the prop, not the substance."
The /connect system exists to prevent that mismatch. It ensures that the same precision and intentionality that went into the card carries through into the first thirty seconds of the digital experience. After those thirty seconds, the visitor is on a real landing page — /provider, /employer, /investor, /partner — that does the substantive convincing work.
Strategic bottom line
The business card buys you trust. The /connect page keeps it. Every existing landing page on usrad.com can then do its job, which is to convert that trust into action.
